---
import OrcaDocs from "../../../../components/OrcaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { ORCA_REPO } from "../../../../data/orca";

const DOCS = `${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/docs`;

const description =
  "Sizing and scaling Orca's runs: requests of CPUs and memory, learned requests, grants and admission, a cgroup per run, memory pressure, max_runs and fill_max_runs set to auto, worker pools that grow themselves, one run over several machines, and which setting to change when.";

const knobs = [
  ["A run is killed for its memory, or waits for room it doesn't need", "memory_gb (a range, or \"auto\")", "#requests", "Requests"],
  ["You don't know what a pipeline needs", "memory_gb = \"auto\", cpus = \"auto\"", "#learned", "Learned requests"],
  ["A pipeline should use more of a bigger host", "a request's maximum, and \"auto\" in env", "#grants", "Grants"],
  ["More runs of one pipeline at once on one host", "max_runs = \"auto\"", "#how-many", "How many at once"],
  ["A fill should go faster on one host", "fill_max_runs = \"auto\" (from 0.24.0)", "#how-many", "How many at once"],
  ["The host stalls although the grants add up", "ORCA_ADMIT_MEMORY_PRESSURE", "#memory-pressure", "Memory pressure"],
  ["The host is full and runs wait", "placement = \"worker:<pool>\" and a second machine", "#pools", "Pools"],
  ["Runs wait only some of the time", "[pools.<name>] with max_machines", "#pools", "Pools"],
  ["One run is too big for one machine", "workers = N on a pool", "#one-run", "One run, several machines"],
];
---

<OrcaDocs href="/s/orca/docs/scaling" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Operations</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Sizing and scaling</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Orca scales in two directions. Vertically, each run is sized to its share of the host
    it runs on. Horizontally, more runs go at once, on more machines, and one run can
    spread over several. This page says how each works and which setting to change.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#vertical">Vertical</a>
    <a href="#memory-pressure">Memory pressure</a>
    <a href="#horizontal">Horizontal</a>
    <a href="#which-knob">Which knob when</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="vertical">Vertical: sizing a run</h2>

  <h3 id="requests">Requests</h3>
  <p>
    <code>cpus</code> and <code>memory_gb</code> are a pipeline's request: the least it
    needs to start and the most it can use, written <code>"MIN..MAX"</code> (a single
    number is both).
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="orca.toml" code={`[pipelines.gharchive]
cpus = "1..16"
memory_gb = "2..24"
env = { DISTA_WORKER_SLOTS = "auto", GHARCHIVE_PARALLEL = "auto" }`} />
  <p>
    A pipeline without a request starts as before: no check and no grant. Its runs aren't
    counted against the others either, so on a shared host give every pipeline a request,
    or keep the host's reserve large enough for the ones without. On EC2 and Kubernetes,
    whose runs get machines of their own, the request is kept but not used yet.
  </p>

  <h3 id="grants">Admission and grants</h3>
  <p>
    On the local runner, for control-placed runs and on each remote worker, a run starts
    only if its minimum fits what the host can grant, less what the runs already going
    there were granted. What a host can grant is its CPUs and memory (its cgroup's limits
    if set; <code>ORCA_HOST_CPUS</code> and <code>ORCA_HOST_MEMORY_MB</code> override
    them), less a reserve: <code>ORCA_RESERVE_CPUS</code> and
    <code>ORCA_RESERVE_MEMORY_MB</code>, 1 CPU and 1536 MB on the scheduler's host and 0
    and 512 MB on a worker. A run that doesn't fit stays queued and says why:
    <code>waiting: needs 2 GB, 1.3 GB free of grants</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A run that starts gets up to its maximum of what is left, in
    <code>ORCA_GRANT_CPUS</code> and <code>ORCA_GRANT_MEMORY_MB</code>. The grant is fixed
    for that start: nothing shrinks or is killed to make room, and a retry is granted
    again. An <code>env</code> value <code>"auto"</code> lets the pipeline size itself
    from its grant (<code>orca.auto_count</code>), and <code>DISTA_WORKER_SLOTS =
    "auto"</code> becomes the granted CPUs over the run's local dista workers.
  </p>

  <h3 id="learned">Learned requests</h3>
  <p>
    From the peaks of a pipeline's last 20 successful measured runs, Orca works out the
    request they suggest: memory is the 95th percentile of their peak memory plus 15%,
    rounded up to a quarter GB; CPUs are the 90th percentile of their peak cores, rounded
    up. With fewer than 3 such runs there is none. <code>memory_gb = "auto"</code> or
    <code>cpus = "auto"</code> uses it, and <code>"auto..24"</code> uses it with 24 as the
    most it may be granted.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="orca.toml" code={`[pipelines.gharchive]
memory_gb = "auto"     # learned from its runs
max_runs = "auto"`} />
  <p>
    Until there is a recommendation, that resource has no request. The pipeline's
    Resources card and <code>orca config show</code> show the recommendation either way
    (<code>learned from 20 runs: 3.25 GB, 2 CPUs</code>), and <code>learn = false</code>
    keeps <code>auto</code> from applying it. Before rolling back to an Orca before
    0.21.0, register without <code>auto</code>: an older Orca refuses it
    (<a href={`${DOCS}/orca-toml.md#learned-requests`} rel="noopener">Learned
    requests</a>).
  </p>

  <h3 id="cgroups">A cgroup per run</h3>
  <p>
    Where Orca can make cgroup v2 cgroups, each local, control-placed and worker run gets
    one. Its grant is the cgroup's <code>memory.high</code>, and
    <code>ORCA_RUN_MEMORY_MARGIN</code> (25) percent above it the kernel kills the run,
    and only that run. A run held at its limit with its tasks stalled on memory for
    <code>ORCA_RUN_MEMORY_HELD_SECONDS</code> (120) is killed by Orca the same way. The
    attempt fails <code>out of memory at &lt;limit&gt;</code>, and the retry and the
    pipeline's later runs ask for <code>ORCA_RUN_MEMORY_GROWTH</code> (1.5) times as
    much, up to <code>memory_gb</code>'s maximum. Killed at that maximum already, the run
    fails without a retry. A run without a request is limited to
    <code>ORCA_RUN_MEMORY_MB</code>, by default what the host can grant. On the Hetzner
    server, <code>orca-stack configure CGROUPS=1</code> turns cgroups on
    (<a href={`${DOCS}/hetzner.md#a-cgroup-per-run`} rel="noopener">hetzner.md</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="memory-pressure">Memory pressure</h2>
  <p>
    Requests are estimates, and a host can run short of memory while its grants still
    add up: page cache, Postgres, a run past its estimate. So on top of the grants, the
    scheduler reads its host's memory pressure every tick, and each worker reads its own
    every poll. This is Linux's pressure stall information (PSI): the share of time tasks
    were stalled waiting for memory.
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The hold.</strong> While <code>some avg10</code> is over
      <code>ORCA_ADMIT_MEMORY_PRESSURE</code> (10 percent; 0 turns it off), no new run
      starts on that host. Runs already going go on. A queued run says
      <code>waiting: memory pressure 23% (limit 10%)</code>, with <code>on worker
      &lt;name&gt;</code> on a pool. The hold ends once the reading is under half the
      limit, or has stayed at most the limit for 30 seconds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Never forever.</strong> A hold that lasts
      <code>ORCA_ADMIT_PRESSURE_GRACE_SECONDS</code> (600) with none of Orca's runs going
      on the host isn't Orca's doing. The scheduler or worker then raises the
      <code>memory-pressure</code> <a href="/s/orca/docs/monitoring#alerts">alert</a>
      once and starts runs there one at a time until the pressure eases.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Which file.</strong> <code>ORCA_MEMORY_PRESSURE_FILE</code> if set; else the
      <code>memory.pressure</code> of the nearest cgroup with a memory limit (a container
      run with <code>--memory</code>); else <code>/proc/pressure/memory</code>. Without
      PSI, as on macOS, nothing is held, and the scheduler says so once.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Runs can read it.</strong> Every run gets
      <code>ORCA_MEMORY_PRESSURE_FILE</code>, and a step can call
      <code>orca.memory_pressure()</code> to lower its own parallelism while it is high.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Ops › Control's Memory pressure card shows the scheduler host's reading, and
    <code>/api/workers</code> each worker's.
  </p>

  <h2 id="horizontal">Horizontal: more runs, more machines</h2>

  <h3 id="how-many">How many at once</h3>
  <p>
    <code>max_runs = "auto"</code> starts another run of the pipeline whenever its request
    fits in what the host's running runs weren't granted, up to a ceiling of 64
    (<code>"auto..N"</code> sets it to N). A bigger host runs more of them at once with no
    change to orca.toml: at 2 GB a run, 4 on a host with 8 GB to grant and 12 on one with
    24. It needs a request to weigh, declared or learned, and starts one run at a time
    until it has one. On EC2, Kubernetes and remote workers, where there is no single
    host's room to weigh, the ceiling is the limit, and each worker still admits what it
    takes against its own host.
  </p>
  <p>
    From 0.24.0, <code>fill_max_runs = "auto"</code> or <code>"auto..N"</code> does the
    same for a pipeline's <a href="/s/orca/docs/run#fills">fill runs</a>, with the same
    ceiling of 64. Before that, <code>fill_max_runs</code> is a count. An older Orca reads
    <code>"auto"</code> as its ceiling.
  </p>

  <h3 id="pools">Pools and autoscaling</h3>
  <p>
    When one host is full, place a pipeline on a pool, <code>placement =
    "worker:&lt;pool&gt;"</code>, and add machines. A pool's workers can be started by
    hand (<a href="/s/orca/docs/workers">Remote workers</a>), or Orca can grow and shrink
    the pool itself from a <code>[pools.&lt;name&gt;]</code> table: it makes a Hetzner
    or EC2 machine while runs wait, up to <code>max_machines</code>, and removes it after
    <code>idle_seconds</code> with nothing to do
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/pools#autoscaling">Pools and autoscaling</a>). On a pool,
    <code>max_runs</code> and <code>fill_max_runs</code> cap the pipeline across all its
    machines, and each machine's grants cap what runs there.
  </p>

  <h3 id="one-run">One run over several machines</h3>
  <p>
    From 0.24.0, <code>workers = N</code> on a pipeline placed on a pool lets one run's dista cluster
    use up to N more machines than the one that starts it. Members join a running run
    when they have room, and on a pool Orca grows, a run that wants more members grows
    the pool (<a href="/s/orca/docs/pools#one-run-many-machines">One run over several
    machines</a>). On the Kubernetes and EC2 runners each run gets a dista cluster of its
    own instead, sized by its registration
    (<a href="/s/orca/docs/run#runners">Runners</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="which-knob">Which knob when</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Change</th><th>See</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {knobs.map(([when, knob, href, label]) => (
          <tr><td>{when}</td><td><code>{knob}</code></td><td class="ref"><a href={href}>{label}</a></td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/config">orca.toml</a> has each key, and
    <a href={`${DOCS}/orca-toml.md#requests-and-grants`} rel="noopener">orca-toml.md</a>
    the full rules.
  </p>
</OrcaDocs>
